-- title:
--   A 1-cocycle vanishing on generators vanishes on the generated subgroup
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a commutative ring, $G$ a group, and $M$ a representation of $G$ over $k$ (an object of `Rep k G`). Let $c$ be an element of `cocycles₁ M`, i.e. a function $G \to M$ satisfying the inhomogeneous $1$-cocycle identity $c(gh) = \rho_M(g)\,c(h) + c(g)$ for all $g, h \in G$, where $c$ is silently coerced to its underlying function. Let $s \subseteq G$ be a subset and assume $c(g) = 0$ for every $g \in s$. Then for every $g$ lying in `Subgroup.closure s`, the subgroup of $G$ generated by $s$, one has $c(g) = 0$. Equivalently: the vanishing locus of a $1$-cocycle is a subgroup of $G$, so vanishing on a generating set of a subgroup propagates to the whole subgroup. Note that no topology, finiteness or continuity hypothesis enters; the statement is purely about the abstract cocycle condition, and $k$, $G$ are taken in the same universe.
--
--   This is the elementary observation that the kernel of a crossed homomorphism $G \to M$ is a subgroup, stated in the form needed to pass from generators to the generated subgroup. It is used in the local Galois-cohomology layer, where a cocycle vanishing on an inertia subgroup and on a level subgroup is shown to vanish on the subgroup they generate, as in [`groupCohomology.exists_cocycles1_unramified_iff_mem_inflationImage_sup`](thm.html#groupCohomology.exists_cocycles1_unramified_iff_mem_inflationImage_sup).
-- source:
